/*  THE MIXING RULE, DECOMPOSED -- the multicomponent lesson.  A cubic
    prices a MIXTURE through two numbers, and the one-fluid rule says how
    n pure components and n(n-1)/2 binary corrections build them:

        a_mix(T) = sum_i sum_j y_i y_j (1 - kij) sqrt(a_i a_j)
        b_mix    = sum_i y_i b_i

    Every term is on the table: the six pair terms of this ternary re-add
    to the a_mix the flash would run (the op REFUSES if they do not), the
    curated N2-CH4 kij sits beside the two CO2 pairs running the announced
    kij = 0 default, and the pair table is where a student sees that a
    ternary needs THREE binary decisions -- there is no ternary parameter.

    Two states on purpose, same composition and temperature: a_mix and
    b_mix are IDENTICAL at 30 bar and 1 bar (the rule sees only T and y),
    while A, B and Z carry the pressure -- at 1 bar the same gas is nearly
    ideal (Z ~ 0.997) and at 30 bar it is not (Z ~ 0.93), priced by the
    SAME two numbers through the cubic.

    Nothing here is validated against measurement: every number is the
    engine's own arithmetic, decomposed.  The one cited constant is the
    N2-CH4 kij record (Knapp et al., DECHEMA VI, 1982).
*/

operations
(
    {
        name        mix_30bar;
        type        mixingRules;
        state
        {
            T            250 K;
            P            30 bar;
            composition  { CH4 0.90;  N2 0.05;  CO2 0.05; }
        }
    }

    {
        name        mix_1bar;
        type        mixingRules;
        state
        {
            T            250 K;
            P            1 bar;
            composition  { CH4 0.90;  N2 0.05;  CO2 0.05; }
        }
    }
);
